Using Count of Set Bits - O(log n) time and O(1) spaceThe idea is to leverage the binary representation of powers of 2, which always have exactly one bit set to 1 (the rest are 0). We can count the number of set bits in the binary representation of n, and if there's exactly one set bit, then n is a power of 2. This works because powers of 2 have the form 2^k, which in binary is a 1 followed by k zeros.
            C++

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

bool isPowerofTwo(int n) {
    if (n <= 0)
        return false;

    // Count set bits
    int count = 0;
    while (n > 0) {
        if (n & 1)
            count++;
        n = n >> 1;
    }

    // If count of set bits is 1, 
    // then n is a power of 2
    return (count == 1);
}

